Z||000003||StuGood||01-25-2001||06:29 PM||||High mileage, doesn't run; since it doesn't run, can't verify lots of stuff.  Like, you can't really observe the A/C working, tranny action, alternator, how it actually runs (how do I know it doesn't overheat in ten minutes), how it drives, handles - way too many unknowns.  Personally, if it's perfect appearance-wise, I'd offer $450.<P>Remember, that's what it'd be worth to <B>me</B>.  And you asked  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.      ||||reg||13
Z||000004||LottaBallsCamaro||01-25-2001||06:31 PM||||I see it run every day and have drove it before...||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||01-25-2001||06:58 PM||||It's probably worth anywhere from $2000-$3000 in good running condition and really is in pretty good shape overall (including paint). Depends partly on where you are and your local market.<P>Right now it's worth that MINUS what it will take to fix it (parts and labor). Labor cost depends heavily if it's your time or you have to pay someone to do it.<P>Remember you are guessing at the timing chain. It could be something easier to fix that you do yourself and you get a great deal... OR, it could be the whole engine and you quickly wind up with more in it than it's worth. <P>Then again, you could buy a cherry one for $3000 and have the motor blow up a month later... Used cars (especially non-runners) are kinda like playing the lottery. How much are you willing (and able) to risk?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||LarryB||01-25-2001||06:59 PM||||Without knowing what is wrong with the engine? I'd say $450 isn't too far off the mark. ||||reg||1
